            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Elementary facts about the two circles  \n\nLet \\(\\Omega\\) (centre \\(M\\), radius \\(r_{\\Omega}\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (centre \\(N\\), radius \\(r_{\\Gamma}>r_{\\Omega}\\)) intersect at the distinct points \\(A,B\\).  \nThe line of centres \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\).\n\nBecause the two circles intersect, the common chord \\(AB\\) is perpendicular to the line of centres; moreover the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(AB\\) lies on \\(MN\\).  \nThus  \n\n\\[\nAB\\perp MN,\\qquad O\\in MN,\\qquad MO=NO. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The spiral similarity with centre \\(P\\)\n\nLet \\(P\\) be the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); hence  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2.1  \n\\(P\\) is the centre of the (unique) spiral similarity that sends the segment \\(CD\\) onto the segment \\(AB\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the quadrilateral \\(ACBD\\). Points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\) which is the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\) by (1); consequently the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(AB\\) is also the midpoint of \\(CD\\).  \n\nSince \\(PA=PC\\) and \\(PD=PA\\) by (2), we have  \n\n\\[\nPC=PA,\\qquad PD=PA.\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{PC}{PD}=1=\\frac{PA}{PB}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad PB=PA. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(P\\) is equidistant from the four points \\(A,B,C,D\\); i.e.  \n\n\\[\nPA=PB=PC=PD. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the four points \\(A,B,C,D\\) are concyclic with centre \\(P\\); denote this circle by \\(\\omega\\).  \n\nBecause \\(O\\) is the midpoint of both chords \\(AB\\) and \\(CD\\) of \\(\\omega\\), the chords are symmetric with respect to the line \\(PO\\). Therefore the rotation about \\(P\\) that carries the ray \\(PC\\) onto the ray \\(PA\\) also carries the ray \\(PD\\) onto the ray \\(PB\\). The similarity is a pure rotation (ratio \\(1\\)), i.e. a **spiral similarity of ratio \\(1\\)** (a rotation) that sends the whole segment \\(CD\\) onto the whole segment \\(AB\\). ∎\n\n\n\nFrom Lemma 2.1 we obtain two immediate angle equalities.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2.2 (Angle‑chasing consequences)\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle BFE\\;},\\qquad\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle (AP,BF)=\\angle BEF\\;}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause the spiral similarity with centre \\(P\\) sends \\(C\\mapsto A\\) and \\(D\\mapsto B\\), it also sends the line \\(CB\\) (a chord of \\(\\omega\\)) onto the line \\(EB\\) (the image of \\(CB\\) under the same similarity). The image of the tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(C\\) is the tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(A\\); the latter is precisely the line \\(AP\\) (since \\(PA\\) is a radius of \\(\\omega\\)).  \n\nHence the oriented angle between \\(AP\\) and \\(EB\\) equals the oriented angle between the tangent at \\(C\\) and the chord \\(CB\\), i.e.  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle BCF .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(B,C,F,E\\) all lie on the same circle \\(\\omega\\), the inscribed‑angle theorem gives \\(\\angle BCF=\\angle BFE\\). This proves the first equality in (5).  \n\nThe second equality is obtained in the same way, using the image of the chord \\(DB\\) (which is \\(FB\\)). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Reflections of the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nRecall the well‑known fact:\n\n> **Fact 3.1** – In any triangle the reflections of the orthocenter across the three sides lie on the circumcircle of the triangle.\n\nApply Fact 3.1 to \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Let  \n\n* \\(H_{M}\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) across side \\(PN\\);  \n* \\(H_{N}\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) across side \\(PM\\).\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\nH_{M},\\;H_{N}\\in (PMN). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(PN\\) and \\(PM\\) are chords of \\((PMN)\\), the segment \\(H_{M}H_{N}\\) is a **diameter** of \\((PMN)\\); indeed the centre of \\((PMN)\\) is the midpoint of \\(H_{M}H_{N}\\) (the nine‑point centre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)). Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\angle H_{M}PH_{N}=90^{\\circ}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Image of the diameter under the spiral similarity\n\nFrom Lemma 2.1 the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) with centre \\(P\\) sends the circle \\((PMN)\\) onto the circle \\((BEF)\\).  \nIndeed \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends the three defining points \\(C,D,A\\) of \\((PMN)\\) to the three defining points \\(E,F,B\\) of \\((BEF)\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S\\bigl((PMN)\\bigr)=(BEF). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nA similarity maps a diameter to a line tangent to the image circle at the image of the opposite endpoint.  \nHence the image of the diameter \\(H_{M}H_{N}\\) is a line tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at the image of the point \\(P\\).  \nBecause \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends \\(P\\) to \\(A\\) (the image of the centre of the similarity is the image of the point fixed by it), the tangent obtained is the line through the image of \\(H\\) that is **parallel to the image of the line \\(MN\\)**.\n\nBut \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends the line \\(MN\\) to the line \\(AB\\); the image of a line parallel to \\(MN\\) is a line parallel to \\(AB\\).  \nSince \\(AP\\) is the image of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(MN\\) (the latter being the line through the orthocenter parallel to the base of the triangle), we conclude that the line through \\(H\\) **parallel to \\(AP\\)** is exactly the image of the diameter \\(H_{M}H_{N}\\).  \n\nBy the property of the image of a diameter, this line is tangent to \\((BEF)\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Completion of the proof  \n\nThe tangency proved in step 4 is equivalent to the two angle equalities (5).  \nThus the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) touches the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
