\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete solution.  \nThe line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Configuration facts** – From the intersecting circles we have  \n   * \\(AB\\perp MN\\) and the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(AB\\) lies on \\(MN\\);  \n   * \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the intersections of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n\n2. **Spiral similarity centred at \\(P\\)** –  \n   * Show that the circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is the centre of the unique spiral similarity that sends the segment \\(CD\\) onto the segment \\(AB\\).  \n   * Consequently the ray \\(AP\\) is the image of the ray \\(MN\\) under this similarity.\n\n3. **Angle‑chasing consequences** – From the spiral similarity we obtain the two equalities  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle BFE\\;},\\qquad\n   \\boxed{\\;\\angle (AP,BF)=\\angle BEF\\;}. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   These are exactly the tangency conditions for a line parallel to \\(AP\\) to be tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n4. **Orthocenter reflections** – In any triangle the reflections of the orthocenter across the sides lie on the circumcircle.  \n   Apply this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and denote by \\(H_{M},H_{N}\\) the reflections of \\(H\\) across \\(PN\\) and \\(PM\\). Then  \n\n   \\[\n   H_{M},H_{N}\\in (PMN)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad H_{M}H_{N}\\text{ is a diameter of }(PMN). \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Image of the diameter** – The spiral similarity of step 2 maps the circle \\((PMN)\\) onto the circle \\((BEF)\\) (because it sends the three defining points \\(C,D,A\\)