\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have found a complete solution.  \nThe line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Inversion at \\(A\\).**  \n   Invert the whole configuration about a circle \\(\\mathcal I\\) centred at \\(A\\) (any radius).  \n   * The two given circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) become two distinct lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) passing through the images \\(C',D'\\) of \\(C,D\\).  \n   * The line \\(MN\\) (which does not pass through \\(A\\)) becomes a circle \\(\\omega\\) passing through \\(A\\) and the images \\(M',N'\\) of \\(M,N\\).  \n\n2. **Images of the special points.**  \n   * Because \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and \\(A\\) is the centre of the inversion, the image \\(P'\\) is the **mid‑point** of the segment \\(C'D'\\).  \n   * The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is sent to the point \\(H'\\) which is the **reflection of \\(P'\\) in the line \\(M'N'\\)** (a standard fact: under an inversion centred at a vertex of a triangle, the orthocentre and the circum‑centre are interchanged after a reflection in the opposite side).  \n\n3. **The image of the required tangent.**  \n   * The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is sent to the line through \\(H'\\) parallel to \\(AP'\\) (parallelism is preserved by an inversion).  \n   * We shall prove that this latter line is tangent to the circumcircle \\((B'E'F')\\) of the images \\(B',E',F'\\) of \\(B,E,F\\).  \n\n4. **A right‑angle configuration.**  \n   * Since \\(B\\) lies on both original circles, its image