the external angle bisector of the angle formed by\n\\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\); consequently\n\n\\[\nB'P'\\perp AP'.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nNow (5) gives \\(\\angle B'P'E'=\\angle B'P'F'=90^{\\circ}\\); hence the\nquadrilateral \\(B'E'F'P'\\) is cyclic and \\(B'P'\\) is a diameter of the\ncircle \\((B'E'F')\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Tangency of \\(\\ell\\) to \\((B'E'F')\\)\n\nBecause \\(B'P'\\) is a diameter, the tangent to \\((B'E'F')\\) at the point\n\\(B'\\) is the line through \\(B'\\) perpendicular to \\(B'P'\\).\nBy (6) this tangent is the line through \\(B'\\) **parallel to \\(AP'\\)**.\n\nFrom (4) the point \\(H'\\) is the reflection of \\(P'\\) in the line\n\\(M'N'\\).  The line \\(M'N'\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the segment\n\\(P'H'\\); therefore the line through \\(H'\\) parallel to \\(AP'\\) meets the\nline through \\(B'\\) parallel to \\(AP'\\) at the same point \\(B'\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\ell\\;=\\;\\text{the line through }H'\\text{ parallel to }AP'\n      \\;=\\;\\text{the tangent to }(B'E'F')\\text{ at }B'.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(B'E'F'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Returning to the original configuration\n\nThe inverse image of the tangent \\(\\ell\\) is exactly the line through the\noriginal orthocentre \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\); the inverse image of\nthe circle \\((B'E'F')\\) is the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) of the original\npoints \\(B,E,F\\).  Therefore, in the original figure,\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{the line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to }(BEF).}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe proof is complete. ∎"
