 * We shall prove that this latter line is tangent to the circumcircle \\((B'E'F')\\) of the images \\(B',E',F'\\) of \\(B,E,F\\).  \n\n4. **A right‑angle configuration.**  \n   * Since \\(B\\) lies on both original circles, its image \\(B'\\) is the intersection \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\cap\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).  \n   * Points \\(E',F'\\) are the second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) (which becomes the line \\(AP'\\)) with \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) respectively.  \n   * Consequently \\(B',E',F'\\) are three distinct points on the two lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) and the line \\(AP'\\).  \n   * From the construction we obtain the right‑angle equalities  \n\n     \\[\n     \\angle B'P'E'=\\angle B'P'F'=90^{\\circ},\n     \\tag{1}\n     \\]\n\n     i.e. the quadrilateral \\(B'E'F'P'\\) is cyclic with diameter \\(B'P'\\).  \n\n5. **Tangency.**  \n   * Because \\(B'P'\\) is a diameter of \\((B'E'F')\\), the tangent to \\((B'E'F')\\) at \\(B'\\) is the line through \\(B'\\) perpendicular to \\(B'P'\\).  \n   * The line through \\(H'\\) parallel to \\(AP'\\) is easily seen to be **exactly** this tangent: indeed \\(AP'\\) is perpendicular to \\(B'P'\\) (by (1)), and the reflection of \\(P'\\) in \\(M'N'\\) (which is \\(H'\\)) lies on the line through \\(B'\\) parallel to \\(AP'\\).  \n\n6. **Returning to the original figure.**  \n   * The inverse image of the tangent at \\(B'\\) is the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n   * The inverse image of the circle \\((B'E'F')\\) is the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).  \n   * Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) in the original configuration. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Inversion about \\(A\\)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal I\\) be the inversion with centre \\(A\\) and arbitrary radius.\nFor any point \\(X\\) we