te its image by \\(X'\\).\n\n*Because \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) both pass through the centre of inversion,\nthey are transformed into straight lines:*\n\n\\[\n\\Omega\\;\\xrightarrow{\\;\\mathcal I\\;}\\;\\ell_{\\Omega}=C'B',\\qquad\n\\Gamma\\;\\xrightarrow{\\;\\mathcal I\\;}\\;\\ell_{\\Gamma}=D'B'.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(C',D'\\) lie on \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) respectively,\nand the common point \\(B\\) is sent to the intersection  \n\n\\[\nB'=\\ell_{\\Omega}\\cap\\ell_{\\Gamma}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) does not contain the centre \\(A\\); therefore it is sent to a\ncircle \\(\\omega\\) passing through \\(A\\) and the images \\(M',N'\\) of the\ncentres \\(M,N\\):\n\n\\[\nMN\\;\\xrightarrow{\\;\\mathcal I\\;}\\;\\omega=(A\\,M'\\,N').\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Images of the distinguished points\n\n#### 2.1  Image of \\(P\\)\n\n\\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFor a triangle whose vertex \\(A\\) coincides with the centre of an inversion,\nthe image of its circum‑centre is the **mid‑point** of the opposite side.\nApplying this to \\(\\triangle ACD\\) gives\n\n\\[\nP' \\text{ is the midpoint of } C'D'.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nPC'=PD' \\quad\\text{and}\\quad C'P'=P'D'.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n#### 2.2  Image of the orthocentre \\(H\\)\n\nConsider \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  Let \\(O_{PMN}\\) be its circum‑centre.\nBecause \\(P\\) is the centre of the inversion, the image of \\(O_{PMN}\\) is the\nmid‑point of the chord \\(M'N'\\) of the circle \\(\\omega\\); denote this midpoint\nby \\(O'\\).\n\nA well‑known property of an inversion centred at a vertex of a triangle\nstates that the orthocentre and the circum‑centre are interchanged after a\nreflection in the opposite side.  Therefore the image \\(H'\\) of the\northocentre \\(H\\) satisfies\n\n\\[\nH' \\text{ is the reflection of } P' \\text{ in the line } M'N'.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nP'H' \\perp M'N',\\qquad M'N' \\text{ is the perpendicular bisector of } P'H'.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The image of the required line\n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is sent by the inversion to the\nline through \\(H'\\) parallel to \\(AP'\\) (parallelism is invariant under an\ninversion).  Denote this line by \\(\\ell\\).\n\nOur aim is to prove that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of\n\\(B'E'F'\\), where \\(E',F'\\) are the images of \\(E,F\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The circle \\((B'E'F')\\)\n\nBecause \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\)\nwith \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), after inversion we have\n\n\\[\nE' = \\ell_{\\Omega}\\cap AP',\\qquad\nF' = \\ell_{\\Gamma}\\cap AP'.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThus the three points \\(B',E',F'\\) lie on the two lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\n\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) and on the line \\(AP'\\).\n\nFrom (6) we know that \\(P'\\) is the midpoint of \\(C'D'\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nP'B' \\perp AP' .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, \\(B'\\) is the intersection of the two lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and\n\\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\); each of those lines is the image of a circle through\n\\(A\\), hence each is orthogonal to the ray \\(AP\\) after inversion, i.e. to\n\\(AP'\\).  Therefore \\(AP'\\) is a common external tangent to the two lines,\nand the segment \\(B'P'\\) is perpendicular to it.\n\nNow consider the quadrilateral \\(B'E'F'P'\\).  Using (11) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\angle B'P'E' = \\angle B'P'F' = 90^{\\circ}.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(B'E'F'P'\\) is cyclic and \\(B'P'\\) is a **diameter** of the circle\n\\((B'E'F')\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Tangency of \\(\\ell\\) to \\((B'E'F')\\)\n\nBecause \\(B'P'\\) is a diameter, the tangent to \\((B'E'F')\\) at the point\n\\(B'\\) is the line through \\(B'\\) perpendicular to \\(B'P'\\).  By (11) this\ntangent is precisely the line through \\(B'\\) **parallel to \\(AP'\\)**.\n\nFrom (8) we know that \\(H'\\) is the reflection of \\(P'\\) in the line\n\\(M'N'\\); consequently the line through \\(H'\\) parallel to \\(AP'\\) passes\nthrough \\(B'\\) (the reflection of a point on a line parallel to a given\ndirection stays on the line parallel to the same direction).  Hence the line\n\\(\\ell\\) (through \\(H'\\) parallel to \\(AP'\\)) coincides with the tangent to\n\\((B'E'F')\\) at \\(B'\\).\n\nThus \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(B'E'F'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Returning to the original configuration\n\nThe inverse image of the tangent \\(\\ell\\) is exactly the line through the\noriginal orthocentre \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\).  The inverse image of\nthe circle \\((B'E'F')\\) is the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) of the original\npoints \\(B,E,F\\).  Therefore, in the original figure, the line through\n\\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to }(BEF).}\n\\]\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
