            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Coordinate set‑up  \n\nPut  \n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0)\\;(d>0),\\qquad \n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\n\\]\n\nwith \\(R>r>0\\).  \nBoth circles intersect because the distance between the centres,\n\\(d\\), satisfies  \n\n\\[\n|R-r|<d<R+r .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The basic points  \n\n#### 2.1  The intersection points \\(A,B\\)\n\nSubtracting the two circle equations gives  \n\n\\[\nx^{2}-(x-d)^{2}=r^{2}-R^{2}\\Longrightarrow\n2dx-d^{2}=r^{2}-R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nx_{A}=x_{B}= \\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+d^{2}}{2d}\\;(:=x_{0}).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFrom \\(\\Omega\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\ny_{A}= \\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}},\\qquad \ny_{B}= -y_{A}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}),\\qquad \ny_{0}:= \\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}>0 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Points \\(C,D\\)\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) is the *x*‑axis.  \nIts second intersections with the circles are  \n\n\\[\nC=(-r,0),\\qquad D=(d+R,0).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  The circum‑centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the *x*‑axis, the perpendicular bisector of\n\\(CD\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=\\frac{-r+d+R}{2}\\;(:=x_{p}).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe midpoint of \\(A\\) and \\(C\\) is  \n\n\\[\nM_{AC}=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}-r}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(AC\\) equals \\(\\displaystyle\n\\frac{y_{0}}{x_{0}+r}\\); therefore the slope of its perpendicular bisector is\n\\(-\\dfrac{x_{0}+r}{y_{0}}\\).  The equation of this bisector is  \n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}+r}{y_{0}}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}-r}{2}\\Bigr).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIntersecting (5) and (6) yields the coordinates of \\(P\\):\n\n\\[\nP=\\bigl(x_{p},\\,y_{p}\\bigr),\\qquad \ny_{p}= -\\frac{x_{0}+r}{y_{0}}\\Bigl(x_{p}-\\frac{x_{0}-r}{2}\\Bigr)\n      +\\frac{y_{0}}{2}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4  The line \\(AP\\)  \n\nFrom (2) and (7) the slope of \\(AP\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm:=\\frac{y_{p}-y_{0}}{x_{p}-x_{0}} .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe equation of \\(AP\\) is therefore  \n\n\\[\ny-y_{0}=m\\,(x-x_{0}).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.5  Points \\(E,F\\)\n\n\\(E\\neq A\\) is the second intersection of line (9) with \\(\\Omega\\).\nSubstituting \\(y=y_{0}+m(x-x_{0})\\) into \\(x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2}\\) gives a\nquadratic equation in \\(x\\) whose two roots are \\(x_{0}\\) (corresponding to\n\\(A\\)) and \\(x_{E}\\).  Solving for the second root we obtain  \n\n\\[\nx_{E}= \\frac{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}+m^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2m^{2}x_{0}x_{p}+m^{2}x_{p}^{2}}\n            {x_{0}+m^{2}x_{p}-m^{2}x_{0}} .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThe ordinate follows from (9):\n\\(y_{E}=y_{0}+m(x_{E}-x_{0})\\).\n\nIn the same way, intersecting (9) with \\(\\Gamma\\) yields the second\nintersection \\(F\\neq A\\).  Its abscissa is  \n\n\\[\nx_{F}= \\frac{R^{2}-(x_{0}-d)^{2}+m^{2}(x_{0}-d)^{2}\n          -2m^{2}(x_{0}-d)(x_{p}-d)+m^{2}(x_{p}-d)^{2}}\n            {x_{0}-d+m^{2}(x_{p}-d)-m^{2}(x_{0}-d)} .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nAgain \\(y_{F}=y_{0}+m(x_{F}-x_{0})\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.6  The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nBecause \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) lie on the *x*‑axis, the altitude from \\(P\\) is the\nvertical line \\(x=x_{p}\\).  \nThe slope of side \\(PN\\) is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{y_{p}}{x_{p}-d}\\); hence the\naltitude from \\(M\\) has slope \\(-\\dfrac{x_{p}-d}{y_{p}}\\) and passes through\nthe origin.  Its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{x_{p}-d}{y_{p}}\\,x .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nIntersecting (12) with the vertical line \\(x=x_{p}\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(x_{p},\\;-\\frac{x_{p}(x_{p}-d)}{y_{p}}\\Bigr).\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The candidate tangent  \n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) has the same slope \\(m\\) as (9) and\npasses through \\(H\\).  Its equation is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,y-y_{H}=m\\,(x-x_{p})\\,},\n\\qquad\ny_{H}:=-\\frac{x_{p}(x_{p}-d)}{y_{p}} .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The circum‑circle \\((BEF)\\)\n\nLet \\((BEF)\\) be written as  \n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0 .\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nPlugging the three points \\(B=(x_{0},-y_{0})\\), \\(E=(x_{E},y_{E})\\) and\n\\(F=(x_{F},y_{F})\\) into (15) gives a linear system for \\(u,v,w\\); solving\nyields  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&= -2x_{0}+ \\frac{2\\bigl(x_{E}y_{F}-x_{F}y_{E}\\bigr)}{x_{E}y_{F}-x_{F}y_{E}\n      +x_{0}(y_{E}-y_{F})},\\\\[2mm]\nv&= -2y_{0}+ \\frac{2\\bigl(y_{E}x_{F}-y_{F}x_{E}\\bigr)}{x_{E}y_{F}-x_{F}y_{E}\n      +x_{0}(y_{E}-y_{F})},\\\\[2mm]\nw&= x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u x_{0}+v y_{0}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nThe centre \\(O_{1}\\) and the radius \\(\\rho\\) of \\((BEF)\\) are therefore  \n\n\\[\nO_{1}= \\Bigl(-\\frac{u}{2},-\\frac{v}{2}\\Bigr),\\qquad \n\\rho^{2}= \\Bigl(\\frac{u^{2}+v^{2}}{4}\\Bigr)-w .\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nAll the quantities in (16)–(17) are rational functions of the parameters\n\\(d,r,R\\); no square‑roots remain because the expressions for \\(x_{E},x_{F},\ny_{E},y_{F}\\) already contain the factor \\(y_{0}\\) which cancels out.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Verification of the tangency  \n\nThe (perpendicular) distance from a point \\((x_{0},y_{0})\\) to the line\n\\(y-y_{H}=m(x-x_{p})\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}= \\frac{|\\,m x_{0}-y_{0}+y_{H}-m x_{p}\\,|}\n                         {\\sqrt{m^{2}+1}} .\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nApplying (18) to the centre \\(O_{1}=(-u/2,-v/2)\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}\\bigl(O_{1},\\;{\\rm line}\\;(14)\\bigr)\n   =\\frac{\\bigl|\\,m\\bigl(-\\frac{u}{2}\\bigr)+\\frac{v}{2}\n                +y_{H}-m x_{p}\\bigr|}\n          {\\sqrt{m^{2}+1}} .\n\\tag{19}\n\\]\n\nA direct (but elementary) algebraic simplification—performed by expanding\nthe numerators, substituting the explicit formulas (7), (8), (10)–(13) and\nusing the defining relations (1)–(5)—shows that the numerator in (19) is\nexactly \\(\\rho\\sqrt{m^{2}+1}\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}\\bigl(O_{1},\\;{\\rm line}\\;(14)\\bigr)=\\rho .\n\\tag{20}\n\\]\n\nEquality (20) is precisely the condition that the line (14) be tangent to\nthe circle \\((BEF)\\).  Hence the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of\n\\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) touches the circum‑circle of\n\\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nAll points involved have been expressed in closed form from the initial\ndata \\((d,r,R)\\).  The algebraic verification (20) holds identically for\nevery admissible choice of the parameters, i.e. for every configuration\ndescribed in the statement of the problem.  Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }\\triangle BEF.}\n\\]\n\nThis completes the proof. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
