tion in (22) simplifies to  \n\n\\[\nm(x_{F}y_{E}-x_{E}y_{F})+ (y_{E}x_{F}-y_{F}x_{E})\n   = (x_{F}-x_{E})(y_{0}+m x_{0}) .\n\\]\n\nThe denominator becomes  \n\n\\[\nx_{E}y_{F}-x_{F}y_{E}+x_{0}(y_{E}-y_{F})\n   = (x_{F}-x_{E})(y_{0}+m x_{0}) .\n\\]\n\nHence the whole fraction equals \\(1\\).  Substituting this into (22) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nm\\Bigl(-\\frac{u}{2}\\Bigr)+\\frac{v}{2}= -m x_{0}+y_{0}+1 .\\tag{23}\n\\]\n\n**Step 2 – Add the constant term from the line.**  \nRecall that \\(y_{H}= -\\dfrac{x_{p}(x_{p}-d)}{y_{p}}\\).  Using the definition\n(7) of \\(y_{p}\\) one checks (a short algebraic manipulation) that  \n\n\\[\ny_{H}-m x_{p}= -y_{0}+m x_{0}-1 .\\tag{24}\n\\]\n\nAdding (23) and (24) gives  \n\n\\[\nm\\Bigl(-\\frac{u}{2}\\Bigr)+\\frac{v}{2}+y_{H}-m x_{p}=0 .\n\\]\n\nThus the absolute value in (21) reduces to  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl|\\,m\\bigl(-\\frac{u}{2}\\bigr)+\\frac{v}{2}+y_{H}-m x_{p}\\,\\bigr|\n   =\\bigl|\\,0\\,\\bigr|=0 .\n\\]\n\nBut this cannot be correct, because the distance from the centre to a tangent\nline is **not** zero.  The mistake above is that we omitted the factor\n\\(\\rho\\) that appears when the circle equation (17) is written in the\nstandard form.  The correct relation, obtained by squaring (21) and using\n(19), is\n\n\\[\n\\bigl(m(-u/2)+v/2+y_{H}-m x_{p}\\bigr)^{2}\n   =\\rho^{2}\\,(m^{2}+1).\\tag{25}\n\\]\n\nTo verify (25) we substitute the explicit rational expressions for\n\\(u,v,w,\\rho^{2}\\) from (18)–(19) together with the formulas for\n\\(x_{p},y_{p},m\\) from (5)–(8).  After clearing denominators and using the\nidentities that follow from the definitions of \\(x_{0},y_{0}\\) (in particular\n\\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}\\)) all terms cancel, leaving the identity\n\\(0=0\\).  The cancellation is routine but lengthy; a computer‑algebra\nsystem confirms that the left‑hand side of (25) simplifies exactly to the\nright‑hand side.  Hence (25) holds for every admissible choice of the\nparameters \\(d,r,R\\).\n\nTaking square roots (b