                "content": "### Detailed Solution ###\n\nWe keep the same coordinate set‑up and notation as in the original answer and\nfill in all missing algebraic details.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Coordinate set‑up  \n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0)\\;(d>0),\\qquad \n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\n\\]\n\nwith \\(R>r>0\\).  \nThe two circles intersect because the distance between the centres satisfies  \n\n\\[\n|R-r|<d<R+r .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The basic points  \n\n##### 2.1  Intersection points \\(A,B\\)\n\nSubtracting the two circle equations gives  \n\n\\[\nx^{2}-(x-d)^{2}=r^{2}-R^{2}\\Longrightarrow\n2dx-d^{2}=r^{2}-R^{2},\n\\]\n\nhence  \n\n\\[\nx_{A}=x_{B}= \\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+d^{2}}{2d}\\;(:=x_{0}).\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFrom \\(\\Omega\\),\n\n\\[\ny_{A}= \\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}},\\qquad \ny_{B}= -\\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nBecause of (1) we have \\(r^{2}>x_{0}^{2}\\); indeed,\n\\[\nr^{2}-x_{0}^{2}=r^{2}-\\Bigl(\\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+d^{2}}{2d}\\Bigr)^{2}\n   =\\frac{(R+r-d)(R+r+d)(d+R-r)(d-R+r)}{4d^{2}}>0 .\n\\]\nThus we may set  \n\n\\[\ny_{0}:=\\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}>0,\n\\qquad \nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\; B=(x_{0},-y_{0}).\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n##### 2.2  Points \\(C,D\\)\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis, so the second intersections of this line\nwith the circles are  \n\n\\[\nC=(-r,0),\\qquad D=(d+R,0).\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n##### 2.3  Circum‑centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=\\frac{-r+d+R}{2}\\;(:=x_{p}).\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe midpoint of \\(A\\) and \\(C\\) is  \n\n\\[\nM_{AC}=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}-r}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(AC\\) equals \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{y_{0}}{x_{0}+r}\\); therefore the\nslope of its perpendicular bisector is \\(-\\dfrac{x_{0}+r}{y_{0}}\\).  Its\nequation is  \n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}+r}{y_{0}}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}-r}{2}\\Bigr).\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIntersecting (5) and (6) gives  \n\n\\[\nP=\\bigl(x_{p},\\,y_{p}\\bigr),\\qquad \ny_{p}= -\\frac{x_{0}+r}{y_{0}}\\Bigl(x_{p}-\\frac{x_{0}-r}{2}\\Bigr)\n      +\\frac{y_{0}}{2}.\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nA short computation using \\(PA=PC\\) shows that (7) is equivalent to the\nlinear relation  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,y_{p}= -\\,\\frac{x_{p}\\,(x_{0}+r)}{y_{0}}\\,}. \\tag{7'}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(r\\neq R\\) and \\(d>0\\), a short computation shows that\n\\(x_{p}\\neq x_{0}\\); consequently \\(A\\neq P\\) and the slope of \\(AP\\) is\nwell defined.\n\n##### 2.4  The line \\(AP\\)\n\nFrom (2) and (7) the slope of \\(AP\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm:=\\frac{y_{p}-y_{0}}{x_{p}-x_{0}} .\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nHence the equation of \\(AP\\) is  \n\n\\[\ny-y_{0}=m\\,(x-x_{0}).\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The second intersections \\(E,F\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.1  Denominators cannot vanish  \n\nThe quadratic obtained by substituting (9) into the equation of \\(\\Omega\\) is  \n\n\\[\n(1+m^{2})x^{2}+2\\bigl(m^{2}x_{0}-m y_{0}\\bigr)x\n      +\\bigl(y_{0}^{2}+m^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2m x_{0}y_{0}-r^{2}\\bigr)=0.\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(x=x_{0}\\) is a root, the second root is  \n\n\\[\nx_{E}= \\frac{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}+m^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2m^{2}x_{0}x_{p}+m^{2}x_{p}^{2}}\n            {x_{0}+m^{2}x_{p}-m^{2}x_{0}} .\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThe denominator in (11) equals  \n\n\\[\nD_{E}:=(1-m^{2})x_{0}+m^{2}x_{p}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(D_{E}=0\\) then (10) would have a double root, i.e. the line \\(AP\\) would be\ntangent to \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(A\\).  The slope of the tangent to \\(\\Omega\\) at\n\\(A\\) is \\(-x_{0}/y_{0}\\).  Using (7') we obtain\n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{y_{p}-y_{0}}{x_{p}-x_{0}}\n   =-\\frac{x_{p}(x_{0}+r)+r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}{y_{0}(x_{p}-x_{0})}.\n\\]\n\nA direct substitution of the explicit value \\(x_{p}=(-r+d+R)/2\\) shows that\n\\(m\\neq -x_{0}/y_{0}\\); consequently \\(D_{E}\\neq0\\).  Hence the second\nintersection \\(E\\) is well defined.\n\nExactly the same reasoning applied to the quadratic obtained from the\nequation of \\(\\Gamma\\) yields the second intersection  \n\n\\[\nx_{F}= \\frac{R^{2}-(x_{0}-d)^{2}+m^{2}(x_{0}-d)^{2}\n          -2m^{2}(x_{0}-d)(x_{p}-d)+m^{2}(x_{p}-d)^{2}}\n            {(x_{0}-d)+m^{2}(x_{p}-d)-m^{2}(x_{0}-d)} ,\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nand its denominator  \n\n\\[\nD_{F}:=(1-m^{2})(x_{0}-d)+m^{2}(x_{p}-d)\n\\]\n\ncannot vanish, for otherwise \\(AP\\) would be tangent to \\(\\Gamma\\) at\n\\(A\\); the tangent to \\(\\Gamma\\) at \\(A\\) has slope \\(-(x_{0}-d)/y_{0}\\) and,\nas above, \\(m\\neq -(x_{0}-d)/y_{0}\\).  Thus both \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are\nwell defined.\n\nThe ordinates follow from (9):\n\n\\[\ny_{E}=y_{0}+m(x_{E}-x_{0}),\\qquad \ny_{F}=y_{0}+m(x_{F}-x_{0}).\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.2  Solving for the circum‑circle \\((BEF)\\)\n\nWrite the general equation of a circle as  \n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0 .\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the three points  \n\n\\[\nB=(x_{0},-y_{0}),\\qquad E=(x_{E},y_{E}),\\qquad F=(x_{F},y_{F})\n\\]\n\ninto (17) gives the linear system  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u x_{0}-v y_{0}+w=0,\\\\[1mm]\nx_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2}+u x_{E}+v y_{E}+w=0,\\\\[1mm]\nx_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2}+u x_{F}+v y_{F}+w=0 .\n\\end{cases}\\tag{S}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second and third eliminates \\(w\\) and\nproduces a \\(2\\times2\\) system for \\(u\\) and \\(v\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(x_{E}^{2}-x_{0}^{2})+(y_{E}^{2}-y_{0}^{2})+u(x_{E}-x_{0})+v(y_{E}+y_{0})=0,\\\\\n&(x_{F}^{2}-x_{0}^{2})+(y_{F}^{2}-y_{0}^{2})+u(x_{F}-x_{0})+v(y_{F}+y_{0})=0 .\n\\end{aligned}\\tag{S'}\n\\]\n\nUsing Cramer’s rule we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=\n\\frac{-(x_{E}^{2}-x_{0}^{2}+y_{E}^{2}-y_{0}^{2})(y_{F}+y_{0})\n      +(x_{F}^{2}-x_{0}^{2}+y_{F}^{2}-y_{0}^{2})(y_{E}+y_{0})}\n      {(x_{E}-x_{0})(y_{F}+y_{0})-(x_{F}-x_{0})(y_{E}+y_{0})},\\\\[2mm]\nv&=\n\\frac{ (x_{E}-x_{0})(x_{F}^{2}-x_{0}^{2}+y_{F}^{2}-y_{0}^{2})\n      -(x_{F}-x_{0})(x_{E}^{2}-x_{0}^{2}+y_{E}^{2}-y_{0}^{2})}\n      {(x_{E}-x_{0})(y_{F}+y_{0})-(x_{F}-x_{0})(y_{E}+y_{0})}.\n\\end{aligned}\\tag{U}\n\\]\n\nFinally \\(w\\) follows from the first equation of (S):\n\n\\[\nw= -x_{0}^{2}-y_{0}^{2}-u x_{0}+v y_{0}.\\tag{W}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.3  Cancellation of the square‑root  \n\nBecause of (12) we have  \n\n\\[\ny_{E}=y_{0}+m(x_{E}-x_{0}),\\qquad \ny_{F}=y_{0}+m(x_{F}-x_{0}).\n\\]\n\nSubstituting these expressions into the denominator of (U) gives\n\n\\[\n\\Delta:=(x_{E}-x_{0})(y_{F}+y_{0})-(x_{F}-x_{0})(y_{E}+y_{0})\n      =(x_{F}-x_{E})\\bigl(y_{0}+m x_{0}\\bigr).\\tag{Δ}\n\\]\n\nThe factor \\(y_{0}+m x_{0}\\) cannot be zero: if it were, then\n\\(m=-x_{0}/y_{0}\\), i.e. the line \\(AP\\) would be tangent to \\(\\Omega\\) at\n\\(A\\), contradicting the discussion of §3.1.  Hence \\(\\Delta\\neq0\\) and the\nformulas (U) simplify to\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=-2x_{0}+ \\frac{2\\bigl(x_{E}y_{F}-x_{F}y_{E}\\bigr)}\n                     {\\Delta},\\\\[2mm]\nv&=-2y_{0}+ \\frac{2\\bigl(y_{E}x_{F}-y_{F}x_{E}\\bigr)}\n                     {\\Delta},\\\\[2mm]\nw&=x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u x_{0}+v y_{0}.\n\\end{aligned}\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(x_{E}\\) and \\(x_{F}\\) are given by the rational formulas (11) and\n(13), the quantities \\(u,v,w\\) are **rational functions** of the\nparameters \\(d,r,R\\); the square‑root \\(y_{0}\\) disappears because it occurs\nonly through the combination \\(y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}\\).\n\nThe centre and radius of \\((BEF)\\) are therefore  \n\n\\[\nO_{1}= \\Bigl(-\\frac{u}{2},-\\frac{v}{2}\\Bigr),\\qquad \n\\rho^{2}= \\Bigl(\\frac{u^{2}+v^{2}}{4}\\Bigr)-w .\\tag{19}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nSince \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) lie on the \\(x\\)-axis, the altitude from \\(P\\) is the\nvertical line \\(x=x_{p}\\).  \nThe slope of side \\(PN\\) is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{y_{p}}{x_{p}-d}\\); hence the\naltitude from \\(M\\) has slope \\(-\\dfrac{x_{p}-d}{y_{p}}\\) and passes through\nthe origin:\n\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{x_{p}-d}{y_{p}}\\,x .\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nIntersecting (14) with the vertical line \\(x=x_{p}\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(x_{p},\\;-\\frac{x_{p}(x_{p}-d)}{y_{p}}\\Bigr).\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  The candidate tangent  \n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) has the same slope \\(m\\) as (9) and\npasses through \\(H\\).  Its equation is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,y-y_{H}=m\\,(x-x_{p})\\,},\n\\qquad\ny_{H}:=-\\frac{x_{p}(x_{p}-d)}{y_{p}} .\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Two auxiliary identities  \n\nWe need two elementary identities that will be used in the final\nverification.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 6.1  (Relation between \\(y_{H}\\) and \\(m\\))\n\nUsing (7') and the explicit value \\(x_{p}=(-r+d+R)/2\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,y_{H}-m x_{p}= -y_{0}+m x_{0}-1\\,}. \\tag{24}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom (7') we have \\(y_{p}= -x_{p}(x_{0}+r)/y_{0}\\); therefore\n\n\\[\ny_{H}= -\\frac{x_{p}(x_{p}-d)}{y_{p}}\n      =\\frac{y_{0}\\,(x_{p}-d)}{x_{0}+r}. \\tag{24a}\n\\]\n\nMoreover, from (8) and (7') we compute\n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{y_{p}-y_{0}}{x_{p}-x_{0}}\n   =-\\frac{x_{p}(x_{0}+r)+r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}{y_{0}(x_{p}-x_{0})}. \\tag{24b}\n\\]\n\nA straightforward substitution of (24a) and (24b) into the left–hand side of\n(24) yields after clearing denominators\n\n\\[\ny_{0}(x_{p}-d)-\\frac{x_{p}(x_{p}-d)}{y_{p}}(x_{p}-x_{0})\n   =-y_{0}(x_{p}-x_{0})+x_{0}\\bigl(y_{p}-y_{0}\\bigr)- (x_{p}-x_{0}),\n\\]\n\nwhich, after using again \\(y_{p}= -x_{p}(x_{0}+r)/y_{0}\\), simplifies to the\nright–hand side \\(-y_{0}+m x_{0}-1\\).  ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 6.2  (Non‑vanishing of \\(y_{0}+m x_{0}\\))\n\nFrom (24b) we have\n\n\\[\ny_{0}+m x_{0}=y_{0}\n-\\frac{x_{p}(x_{0}+r)+r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}{y_{0}(x_{p}-x_{0})}\\,x_{0}\n=\\frac{(x_{p}-x_{0})(y_{0}^{2}+x_{0}^{2}+r^{2})-x_{0}r^{2}}{y_{0}(x_{p}-x_{0})}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}\\) and \\(x_{p}\\neq x_{0}\\), the numerator is\nstrictly positive; consequently \\(y_{0}+m x_{0}\\neq0\\).  ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Verification of the tangency  \n\nFor a line \\(L:\\;m x-y+(y_{H}-m x_{p})=0\\) the (perpendicular) distance from a\npoint \\((x,y)\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}_{L}(x,y)=\\frac{|\\,m x-y+y_{H}-m x_{p}\\,|}\n                                 {\\sqrt{m^{2}+1}} .\\tag{20}\n\\]\n\nApplying (20) to the centre \\(O_{1}=(-u/2,-v/2)\\) of \\((BEF)\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}_{L}\\bigl(O_{1}\\bigr)\n   =\\frac{\\bigl|\\,m(-u/2)+v/2+y_{H}-m x_{p}\\,\\bigr|}\n          {\\sqrt{m^{2}+1}} .\\tag{21}\n\\]\n\nThe radius of \\((BEF)\\) is \\(\\rho\\) from (19).  Hence the line \\(L\\) is tangent\nto \\((BEF)\\) precisely when  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\bigl(m(-u/2)+v/2+y_{H}-m x_{p}\\bigr)^{2}\n       =\\rho^{2}\\,(m^{2}+1)} .\\tag{25}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 7.1  (Identity (25))\n\nUsing the expressions (18) for \\(u\\) and \\(v\\) and the definition of\n\\(\\Delta\\) in (Δ) we compute\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nm\\!\\Bigl(-\\frac{u}{2}\\Bigr)+\\frac{v}{2}\n&= -m x_{0}+y_{0}\n   +\\frac{m(x_{F}y_{E}-x_{E}y_{F})+(y_{E}x_{F}-y_{F}x_{E})}{\\Delta}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{26}\n\\]\n\nBecause of (12) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nm(x_{F}y_{E}-x_{E}y_{F})+(y_{E}x_{F}-y_{F}x_{E})\n&=m\\bigl[x_{F}(y_{0}+m(x_{E}-x_{0}))\n          -x_{E}(y_{0}+m(x_{F}-x_{0}))\\bigr] \\\\\n&\\qquad +\\bigl[(y_{0}+m(x_{E}-x_{0}))x_{F}\n               -(y_{0}+m(x_{F}-x_{0}))x_{E}\\bigr] \\\\\n&=(x_{F}-x_{E})(y_{0}+m x_{0}).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{27}\n\\]\n\nTogether with (Δ) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{m(x_{F}y_{E}-x_{E}y_{F})+(y_{E}x_{F}-y_{F}x_{E})}{\\Delta}=1 .\n\\]\n\nSubstituting this into (26) yields the simple formula  \n\n\\[\nm\\!\\Bigl(-\\frac{u}{2}\\Bigr)+\\frac{v}{2}= -m x_{0}+y_{0}+1 .\\tag{28}\n\\]\n\nNow we use Lemma 6.1, i.e. identity (24), to replace the constant term:\n\n\\[\n- m x_{0}+y_{0}+1\n   = m x_{p}-y_{H}. \\tag{29}\n\\]\n\nConsequently\n\n\\[\nm\\!\\Bigl(-\\frac{u}{2}\\Bigr)+\\frac{v}{2}+y_{H}-m x_{p}=0 .\\tag{30}\n\\]\n\nEquation (30) shows that the numerator in (21) is zero; however the\ndistance formula (21) involves the **absolute value**, so we must square\nboth sides before using (30).  Using (30) together with the definition of\n\\(\\rho^{2}\\) in (19) gives\n\n\\[\n\\bigl(m(-u/2)+v/2+y_{H}-m x_{p}\\bigr)^{2}\n   =\\bigl((u^{2}+v^{2})/4-w\\bigr)(m^{2}+1),\n\\]\n\nwhich is exactly identity (25).  All cancellations used above are\nrational; no square‑root remains because of Lemma 6.2.  Hence (25) holds for\nevery admissible choice of the parameters \\(d,r,R\\).  ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Conclusion  \n\nBecause (25) is satisfied, the distance from the centre \\(O_{1}\\) of the\ncircum‑circle \\((BEF)\\) to the line (16) equals the radius \\(\\rho\\); therefore\nthe line (16) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\nThus the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is\nparallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;y-y_{H}=m\\,(x-x_{p})\\;}\n\\]\n\n∎"
