ssion (23) for \\(y_{H}-m x_{p}\\) is presented without any intermediate algebra; the derivation is omitted.\n\n#### Lemma 6.2  \n\n> “Using (8) and (7), … (24). … It remains to show that \\(2x_{p}-3x_{0}\\neq0\\). … The right‑hand side is a quadratic in \\(d\\) … its larger root is \\(d_{*}= (R-r)+\\sqrt{(R-r)(4R+2r)}\\). … consequently \\(d\\) never reaches the root and the quadratic remains positive. Hence \\(2x_{p}-3x_{0}>0\\) and (24) yields … (26).”\n\n**Justification Gap:**  \n1. The algebra leading to (24) is omitted.  \n2. The inequality argument for \\(2x_{p}-3x_{0}\\neq0\\) is sketched but not rigorously proved; the claim that the quadratic is positive for all admissible \\(d\\) needs a detailed sign analysis.  \n3. The positivity of the denominator \\(y_{0}(x_{p}-x_{0})\\) is asserted based on “\\(x_{p}>x_{0}\\)”, which itself is not proved.\n\n#### Lemma 6.3  \n\n> “Assume, for a contradiction, that \\(x_{E}=x_{F}\\). … the line \\(AP\\) would be tangent to both circles at the same point, which is impossible because the circles intersect at the distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\). … therefore \\(x_{E}\\neq x_{F}\\).”\n\n**Justification Gap:** The argument that a common second intersection would force tangency is not fully justified; one must show that the line would have to be tangent to each circle at that common point, which requires a separate argument. The proof is informal.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Verification of the tangency  \n\n> Distance formula (27) and condition (29) are standard.\n\n*Correct.*\n\n#### Lemma 7.1  \n\n> “Using the simplified expressions (18) we compute … after a straightforward substitution … we obtain … (33).”\n\n**Justification Gap:** The derivation of (33) from the massive algebraic expressions is omitted. The cancellation of the square‑root \\(y_{0}\\) relies on Lemma 6.2, but the explicit steps are not shown. This is the pivotal identity that proves tangency; without a detailed verification the proof remains incomplete.\n\n---\n