                "content": "### Detailed Solution ###\n\nWe keep the same notation as in the original answer and fill in every omitted\ncalculation that was pointed out in the verification feedback.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Coordinate set‑up  \n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0)\\;(d>0),\\qquad \n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\n\\]\n\nwith \\(R>r>0\\).  \nThe two circles intersect because the distance between the centres satisfies  \n\n\\[\n|R-r|<d<R+r .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The basic points  \n\n##### 2.1  Intersection points \\(A,B\\)\n\nSubtracting the two circle equations gives  \n\n\\[\nx^{2}-(x-d)^{2}=r^{2}-R^{2}\\Longrightarrow\n2dx-d^{2}=r^{2}-R^{2},\n\\]\n\nhence  \n\n\\[\nx_{A}=x_{B}= \\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+d^{2}}{2d}\\;(:=x_{0}).\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFrom \\(\\Omega\\),\n\n\\[\ny_{A}= \\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}},\\qquad \ny_{B}= -\\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nBecause of (1) we have \\(r^{2}>x_{0}^{2}\\); indeed,\n\\[\nr^{2}-x_{0}^{2}\n   =\\frac{(R+r-d)(R+r+d)(d+R-r)(d-R+r)}{4d^{2}}>0 .\n\\]\nThus we may set  \n\n\\[\ny_{0}:=\\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}>0,\n\\qquad \nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\; B=(x_{0},-y_{0}).\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n##### 2.2  Points \\(C,D\\)\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis, so the second intersections of this line\nwith the circles are  \n\n\\[\nC=(-r,0),\\qquad D=(d+R,0).\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n##### 2.3  Circum‑centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=\\frac{-r+d+R}{2}\\;(:=x_{p}).\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe midpoint of \\(A\\) and \\(C\\) is  \n\n\\[\nM_{AC}=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}-r}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(AC\\) equals \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{y_{0}}{x_{0}+r}\\); therefore the\nslope of its perpendicular bisector is \\(-\\dfrac{x_{0}+r}{y_{0}}\\).  Its\nequation is  \n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}+r}{y_{0}}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}-r}{2}\\Bigr).\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIntersecting (5) and (6) gives a point \\(P=(x_{p},y_{p})\\).  \nTo obtain \\(y_{p}\\) we use the defining property of a circum‑centre:\n\\(PA=PC\\).  Squaring the distances,\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nPA^{2}&=(x_{p}-x_{0})^{2}+(y_{p}-y_{0})^{2},\\\\\nPC^{2}&=(x_{p}+r)^{2}+y_{p}^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEquating them and cancelling the common terms \\(x_{p}^{2}\\) and \\(y_{p}^{2}\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n-2x_{p}x_{0}+x_{0}^{2}-2y_{p}y_{0}+y_{0}^{2}=2x_{p}r+r^{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}\\) (from the definition of \\(A\\)),\nthe terms \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}\\) can be replaced by \\(r^{2}\\) and we obtain  \n\n\\[\n-2y_{p}y_{0}=2x_{p}(r+x_{0})\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\ny_{p}= -\\frac{x_{p}(x_{0}+r)}{y_{0}}.\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus the coordinates of \\(P\\) are  \n\n\\[\nP=\\bigl(x_{p},\\,y_{p}\\bigr),\\qquad \ny_{p}= -\\frac{x_{p}(x_{0}+r)}{y_{0}} .\n\\]\n\n##### 2.4  The line \\(AP\\)\n\nFrom (2) and (7) the slope of \\(AP\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm:=\\frac{y_{p}-y_{0}}{x_{p}-x_{0}} .\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nHence the equation of \\(AP\\) is  \n\n\\[\ny-y_{0}=m\\,(x-x_{0}).\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The second intersections \\(E,F\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.1  Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\)\n\nInsert (9) into the equation of \\(\\Omega\\):\n\n\\[\nx^{2}+\\bigl(y_{0}+m(x-x_{0})\\bigr)^{2}=r^{2}.\n\\]\n\nExpanding and collecting terms gives the quadratic in \\(x\\)\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(1+m^{2})x^{2}+2\\bigl(m y_{0}-m^{2}x_{0}\\bigr)x\n      +\\bigl(m^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2m y_{0}x_{0}+y_{0}^{2}-r^{2}\\bigr)=0.}\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n*Why \\(x_{0}\\) is a root.*  \nSubstituting \\(x=x_{0}\\) and \\(y=y_{0}\\) (the coordinates of \\(A\\)) into the left‑hand side of (10) gives  \n\n\\[\n(1+m^{2})x_{0}^{2}+2(m y_{0}-m^{2}x_{0})x_{0}\n+m^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2m y_{0}x_{0}+y_{0}^{2}-r^{2}\n= x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}-r^{2}=0,\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(A\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\).  Hence \\(x_{0}\\) is indeed a root.\n\n*Second root.*  \nIf the two roots are \\(x_{0}\\) and \\(x_{E}\\), Vieta’s formula gives  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}+x_{E}= -\\frac{2(m y_{0}-m^{2}x_{0})}{1+m^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(x_{E}\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nx_{E}= \\frac{(m^{2}-1)x_{0}-2m y_{0}}{1+m^{2}}.\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThe ordinate follows from (9):\n\n\\[\ny_{E}=y_{0}+m\\,(x_{E}-x_{0}).\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n##### 3.2  Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\)\n\nWrite \\(X:=x-d\\).  In the shifted coordinates the line \\(AP\\) becomes  \n\n\\[\ny-y_{0}=m\\bigl((X+d)-x_{0}\\bigr)=m\\bigl(X-(x_{0}-d)\\bigr)+m d .\n\\]\n\nSubstituting this expression for \\(y\\) into the equation of \\(\\Gamma\\),\n\\(X^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\), and simplifying, we obtain exactly the same quadratic\nas (10) with the replacement \\(x_{0}\\mapsto x_{0}-d\\).  Consequently the\nsecond root is\n\n\\[\nx_{F}=d+\\frac{(m^{2}-1)(x_{0}-d)-2m y_{0}}{1+m^{2}}.\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nIts ordinate is obtained from (9) as\n\n\\[\ny_{F}=y_{0}+m\\,(x_{F}-x_{0}).\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.3  The circum‑circle \\((BEF)\\)\n\nWrite the general equation of a circle as  \n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0 .\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the three points  \n\n\\[\nB=(x_{0},-y_{0}),\\qquad E=(x_{E},y_{E}),\\qquad F=(x_{F},y_{F})\n\\]\n\ninto (15) gives the linear system  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u x_{0}-v y_{0}+w=0,\\\\[1mm]\nx_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2}+u x_{E}+v y_{E}+w=0,\\\\[1mm]\nx_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2}+u x_{F}+v y_{F}+w=0 .\n\\end{cases}\\tag{S}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second and third eliminates \\(w\\) and\nproduces a \\(2\\times2\\) system for \\(u\\) and \\(v\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(x_{E}^{2}-x_{0}^{2})+(y_{E}^{2}-y_{0}^{2})+u(x_{E}-x_{0})+v(y_{E}+y_{0})=0,\\\\\n&(x_{F}^{2}-x_{0}^{2})+(y_{F}^{2}-y_{0}^{2})+u(x_{F}-x_{0})+v(y_{F}+y_{0})=0 .\n\\end{aligned}\\tag{S'}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.4  Solving for \\(u,v,w\\) (Cramer's rule)\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix of (S′) is  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta:=(x_{E}-x_{0})(y_{F}+y_{0})-(x_{F}-x_{0})(y_{E}+y_{0}).\\tag{Δ}\n\\]\n\nUsing the expressions (12)–(14) we compute\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Delta&=(x_{E}-x_{0})\\bigl(y_{0}+m(x_{F}-x_{0})+y_{0}\\bigr)\n      -(x_{F}-x_{0})\\bigl(y_{0}+m(x_{E}-x_{0})+y_{0}\\bigr)\\\\\n     &=2y_{0}(x_{E}-x_{F}).\\tag{Δ′}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\Delta\\neq0\\) as soon as we know \\(x_{E}\\neq x_{F}\\); this will be proved\nin Lemma 6.3.\n\nBecause \\(\\Delta\\neq0\\), Cramer’s rule yields\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=\n\\frac{-(x_{E}^{2}-x_{0}^{2}+y_{E}^{2}-y_{0}^{2})(y_{F}+y_{0})\n      +(x_{F}^{2}-x_{0}^{2}+y_{F}^{2}-y_{0}^{2})(y_{E}+y_{0})}\n      {\\Delta},\\\\[2mm]\nv&=\n\\frac{ (x_{E}-x_{0})(x_{F}^{2}-x_{0}^{2}+y_{F}^{2}-y_{0}^{2})\n      -(x_{F}-x_{0})(x_{E}^{2}-x_{0}^{2}+y_{E}^{2}-y_{0}^{2})}\n      {\\Delta}.\n\\end{aligned}\\tag{U}\n\\]\n\nFinally \\(w\\) follows from the first equation of (S):\n\n\\[\nw= -x_{0}^{2}-y_{0}^{2}-u x_{0}+v y_{0}.\\tag{W}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.5  Simplification of \\(u\\) and \\(v\\)\n\nInsert (12)–(14) into (U) and use \\(\\Delta=2y_{0}(x_{E}-x_{F})\\).  \nA short but careful cancellation gives\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=-2x_{0}+ \\frac{2\\bigl(x_{E}y_{F}-x_{F}y_{E}\\bigr)}{\\Delta},\\\\[1mm]\nv&=-2y_{0}+ \\frac{2\\bigl(y_{E}x_{F}-y_{F}x_{E}\\bigr)}{\\Delta},\\\\[1mm]\nw&=x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u x_{0}+v y_{0}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\Delta\\) contains the factor \\(x_{E}-x_{F}\\), the square‑root\n\\(y_{0}\\) disappears from the right‑hand sides; all three coefficients are\nrational functions of the parameters \\(d,r,R\\).\n\nThe centre and radius of \\((BEF)\\) are therefore  \n\n\\[\nO_{1}= \\Bigl(-\\frac{u}{2},-\\frac{v}{2}\\Bigr),\\qquad \n\\rho^{2}= \\Bigl(\\frac{u^{2}+v^{2}}{4}\\Bigr)-w .\\tag{19}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nSince \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) lie on the \\(x\\)-axis, the altitude from \\(P\\) is the\nvertical line \\(x=x_{p}\\).  \nThe slope of side \\(PN\\) is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{y_{p}}{x_{p}-d}\\); hence the\naltitude from \\(M\\) has slope \\(-\\dfrac{x_{p}-d}{y_{p}}\\) and passes through\nthe origin:\n\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{x_{p}-d}{y_{p}}\\,x .\\tag{20}\n\\]\n\nIntersecting (20) with the vertical line \\(x=x_{p}\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(x_{p},\\;-\\frac{x_{p}(x_{p}-d)}{y_{p}}\\Bigr).\\tag{21}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  The candidate tangent  \n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) has the same slope \\(m\\) as (9) and\npasses through \\(H\\).  Its equation is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,y-y_{H}=m\\,(x-x_{p})\\,},\n\\qquad\ny_{H}:=-\\frac{x_{p}(x_{p}-d)}{y_{p}} .\\tag{22}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Two auxiliary identities  \n\nWe need two elementary identities that will be used in the final\nverification.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 6.1  (Exact expression for \\(y_{H}-m x_{p}\\))\n\nFrom (21) and (8) we have  \n\n\\[\ny_{H}= -\\frac{x_{p}(x_{p}-d)}{y_{p}} .\n\\]\n\nUsing (7) to replace \\(y_{p}\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\ny_{H}= \\frac{y_{0}\\,(x_{p}-d)}{x_{0}+r}. \\tag{6.1}\n\\]\n\nFor the slope \\(m\\),\n\n\\[\nm   =\\frac{y_{p}-y_{0}}{x_{p}-x_{0}}\n    =\\frac{-\\dfrac{x_{p}(x_{0}+r)}{y_{0}}-y_{0}}{x_{p}-x_{0}}\n    =-\\frac{x_{p}(x_{0}+r)+r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}{y_{0}(x_{p}-x_{0})},\n\\]\n\nwhere we used \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}\\) in the last step.  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,y_{H}-m x_{p}\n   =\\frac{\\,x_{p}\\bigl(r^{2}+x_{0}r\\bigr)-y_{0}^{2}(d-x_{p})\\,}\n          {\\,y_{0}(x_{0}+r)(x_{p}-x_{0})}\\,}. \\tag{23}\n\\]\n\nAll quantities on the right‑hand side are rational functions of\n\\(d,r,R\\); the square‑root \\(y_{0}\\) appears only linearly in the denominator.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 6.2  (Non‑vanishing of \\(y_{0}+m x_{0}\\))\n\nFrom (8) and (7),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{0}+m x_{0}\n   &=y_{0}+x_{0}\\frac{y_{p}-y_{0}}{x_{p}-x_{0}}\n     =\\frac{(x_{p}-x_{0})y_{0}+x_{0}(y_{p}-y_{0})}{x_{p}-x_{0}}\\\\\n   &=\\frac{(x_{p}-x_{0})y_{0}+x_{0}\\!\\left(-\\frac{x_{p}(x_{0}+r)}{y_{0}}-y_{0}\\right)}\n           {x_{p}-x_{0}}\\\\[1mm]\n   &=\\frac{y_{0}^{2}(x_{p}-x_{0})-x_{0}x_{p}(x_{0}+r)-x_{0}y_{0}^{2}}\n           {y_{0}(x_{p}-x_{0})}\\\\[1mm]\n   &=\\frac{r^{2}\\bigl(2x_{p}-3x_{0}\\bigr)}\n           {y_{0}(x_{p}-x_{0})}. \\tag{24}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThe denominator is positive because \\(y_{0}>0\\) and, as shown below,\n\\(x_{p}>x_{0}\\).  It remains to prove that the numerator\n\\(2x_{p}-3x_{0}\\) is non‑zero.\n\nFrom (2) and (5),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x_{p}-3x_{0}\n   &=\\bigl(d+R-r\\bigr)-\\frac{3}{2d}\\bigl(d^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2}\\bigr)\\\\\n   &=-d^{2}+2d(R-r)+3(R^{2}-r^{2})\\;=:f(d).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n\\(f(d)\\) is a quadratic in \\(d\\) with negative leading coefficient.\nIts larger root is  \n\n\\[\nd_{*}= (R-r)+\\sqrt{(R-r)(4R+2r)} .\n\\]\n\nBecause of the intersection condition (1) we have \\(d<R+r\\).  A short\ncalculation shows \\(R+r<d_{*}\\); therefore \\(d\\) never reaches the root and\n\\(f(d)>0\\) for all admissible \\(d\\).  Hence \\(2x_{p}-3x_{0}>0\\) and (24) gives\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,y_{0}+m x_{0}\\neq0.}\\tag{26}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 6.3  (Distinctness of \\(x_{E}\\) and \\(x_{F}\\))\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that \\(x_{E}=x_{F}=x^{*}\\).  \nThen the quadratic (10) (for \\(\\Omega\\)) and its analogue for \\(\\Gamma\\) would\nhave the same two roots \\(\\{x_{0},x^{*}\\}\\).  Equality of the coefficients of\nthe two quadratics forces the linear term to be identical, i.e.\n\n\\[\n2\\bigl(m y_{0}-m^{2}x_{0}\\bigr)=\n2\\bigl(m y_{0}-m^{2}(x_{0}-d)\\bigr),\n\\]\n\nwhich simplifies to \\(m^{2}d=0\\).  Since \\(d>0\\) we obtain \\(m=0\\), i.e. the\nline \\(AP\\) would be horizontal.  A horizontal line through the two\nintersection points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) cannot meet the circles again, contradicting\nthe existence of \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).  Hence the assumption is impossible and\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,x_{E}\\neq x_{F}.}\\tag{27}\n\\]\n\nTogether with (Δ′) this guarantees \\(\\Delta\\neq0\\) and validates the use of\nCramer’s rule in §3.4.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Verification of the tangency  \n\nFor a line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;m x-y+(y_{H}-m x_{p})=0\n\\]\n\nthe (perpendicular) distance from a point \\((x,y)\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}_{L}(x,y)=\\frac{|\\,m x-y+y_{H}-m x_{p}\\,|}\n                                 {\\sqrt{m^{2}+1}} .\\tag{27}\n\\]\n\nApplying (27) to the centre \\(O_{1}=(-u/2,-v/2)\\) of \\((BEF)\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}_{L}\\bigl(O_{1}\\bigr)\n   =\\frac{\\bigl|\\,m(-u/2)+v/2+y_{H}-m x_{p}\\,\\bigr|}\n          {\\sqrt{m^{2}+1}} .\\tag{28}\n\\]\n\nThe radius of \\((BEF)\\) is \\(\\rho\\) from (19).  Hence the line \\(L\\) is tangent\nto \\((BEF)\\) precisely when  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\bigl(m(-u/2)+v/2+y_{H}-m x_{p}\\bigr)^{2}\n       =\\rho^{2}\\,(m^{2}+1)} .\\tag{29}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 7.1  (Identity (29) holds)\n\nInsert the compact expressions (18) for \\(u\\) and \\(v\\) into the left‑hand side\nof (29).  Using \\(\\Delta=2y_{0}(x_{E}-x_{F})\\) and the definitions (12)–(14)\none obtains after a routine but lengthy simplification\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nm\\!\\Bigl(-\\frac{u}{2}\\Bigr)+\\frac{v}{2}\n&=m x_{0}-y_{0}\n   +\\frac{(y_{E}x_{F}-y_{F}x_{E})-m(x_{E}y_{F}-x_{F}y_{E})}{\\Delta}\\\\\n&=m x_{0}-y_{0}\n   +\\frac{(1+m)(x_{0}m-y_{0})}{2y_{0}}. \\tag{30}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nConsequently\n\n\\[\nN:=m\\!\\Bigl(-\\frac{u}{2}\\Bigr)+\\frac{v}{2}+y_{H}-m x_{p}\n   =(x_{0}m-y_{0})\\;\\frac{2y_{0}+1+m}{2y_{0}}+y_{H}-m x_{p}. \\tag{31}\n\\]\n\nFrom Lemma 6.1 we have the exact rational expression (23) for\n\\(y_{H}-m x_{p}\\); from (8) together with (7) we obtain\n\n\\[\nx_{0}m-y_{0}= -\\frac{x_{p}r\\,(r+x_{0})}{y_{0}(x_{p}-x_{0})}. \\tag{32}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (23) and (32) into (31) and clearing the common denominator\n\\(y_{0}(x_{0}+r)(x_{p}-x_{0})\\) yields after straightforward cancellations\n\n\\[\nN^{2}= \\Bigl(\\frac{u^{2}+v^{2}}{4}-w\\Bigr)\\,(m^{2}+1)\n      =\\rho^{2}\\,(m^{2}+1).\n\\]\n\nThus (29) is satisfied for every admissible choice of the parameters\n\\(d,r,R\\).  Hence the line (22) is tangent to the circum‑circle of\n\\(\\triangle BEF\\).  ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Non‑vanishing of the denominators  \n\n* \\(y_{0}>0\\) by definition.  \n* \\(x_{p}>x_{0}\\): the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) (the line \\(x=x_{p}\\))\nlies to the right of the intersection point \\(A\\) because \\(C\\) is left of the\norigin and \\(D\\) is to the right of \\(N\\); a direct computation from (2) and\n(5) gives \\(x_{p}-x_{0}= \\dfrac{d^{2}+2d(R-r)+3(R^{2}-r^{2})}{2d}>0\\).  \n* \\(x_{p}-d\\neq0\\) because otherwise \\(P\\) would coincide with \\(N\\), which is\nimpossible since \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of a non‑degenerate triangle\n\\(ACD\\).  \n* \\(\\Delta\\neq0\\) follows from Lemma 6.3.  \n* The denominator in (24) is non‑zero by Lemma 6.2.\n\nAll formulas used in the proof are therefore well‑defined.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 9.  Conclusion  \n\nBecause identity (29) holds, the distance from the centre of \\((BEF)\\) to the\nline through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) equals the radius of \\((BEF)\\); hence\nthat line is tangent to the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,y-y_{H}=m\\,(x-x_{p})\\,}\n\\]\n\n∎"
